995:## Standard Library
996-
997-### console
998-
999-```redblue
1000-say "Hello"                      // print with a newline
1001-print "Hello"                    // print without one
1002-console.log("Hello")             // `say` under its module spelling
1003-console.error("Could not read")  // diagnostics go to stderr
1004-console.clear()                  // clear the screen
1005-set name to input("Your name? ")  // read one line from the terminal
1006-```
1007-
1008-`say` and `print` are keywords rather than module functions, so there is no
1009-`console.say` and no `console.print`: a module has the functions that are spelled
1010-`<module>_<member>` in the registry, and those two are not. `console.log` is the
1011-module spelling of `say` and answers `nothing`, so `say console.log("x")` prints
1012-`x` and then `nothing`. `console.error` writes to stderr rather than stdout, so a
1013-program that collects its output can keep its diagnostics separate from it.
1014-`console.clear` writes the terminal's clear sequence to stdout and answers
1015-`nothing`; on a stream that is not a terminal the sequence is still written.
1016-
1017-`input` is the whole of the language's input: it writes its first argument as a
1018-prompt, reads one line from the terminal, and answers that line as text with the
1019-line terminator removed — `"abc"` from `abc\n` and from `abc` alike, since a pipe
1020-whose last line has no newline is still a line. At the end of the input it
1021-answers the empty text rather than refusing, so a program that runs out of input
1022-sees an empty answer instead of an error. The prompt is optional:
1023-`input()` reads a line and asks nothing. It is a registered builtin rather than a
1024-module function, so there is no `console.input` — the same rule as for `say` and
1025-`print` — and it takes parentheses like any other call.
1026-
1027-There is no `ask`. It was a reserved word with no rule in the parser to read it,
1028-which is how this section came to name it, and it is an ordinary identifier
1029-again — `set ask to 1` is a program. Both spellings are refused: `ask("n")` is
1030-`Unknown function 'ask'`, and `ask "n"` — which is not a call at all, because a
1031-function is only called with parentheses — is `Unknown variable 'ask'`.
1032-
1033-### text
1034-
1035-```redblue
1036-set upper to text.uppercase("hello")  // "HELLO"
1037-set lower to text.lowercase("HELLO")  // "hello"
1038-set parts to text.split("a,b,c", by ",")  // ["a", "b", "c"]
1039-set joined to text.join(["a", "b", "c"], by ",")  // "a,b,c"
1040-```
1041-
1042-`length` counts **bytes**, which is what a text is made of: `length("héllo")` is
1043-6 and `length("世界")` is 6, not 2. The same number is spelled as a list by
1044-`length(bytes.from_text("héllo"))`, so a program that wants the byte values
1045-rather than the count asks § bytes below for them. `tests/test_text.rb` pins this
1046-in six tests and `corpus/unicode-0001..0008` record it, including the case the
1047-rule exists for: `é` — an `e` and a combining acute accent — is one glyph, three
1048-bytes and a length of 3.
1049-
1050-### math
1051-
1052-```redblue
1053-set pi to PI
1054-set e to E
1055-set sqrt2 to math.sqrt(2)
1056-set rounded to math.round(3.7)  // 4
1057-set powered to math.pow(2, 10)  // 1024
1058-set floored to math.floor(1.7)  // 1
1059-```
1060-
1061-`PI` and `E` are globals, not module members: a module has functions, so
1062-`math.PI` is not a name in the language.
1063-
1064-`math.random` is not implemented. The random builtins are spelled flat: `random`,
1065-`random_seed`, `random_number`, `random_choice` and `random_shuffle`.
1066-
1067-```redblue
1068-random_seed(42)                // every draw after this is reproducible
1069-set n to random(1, 6)          // a whole number from 1 to 6, both ends included
1070-set f to random_number(0, 1)   // a number from 0 up to (not including) 1
1071-set one to random_choice(xs)    // one member of a list
1072-set mixed to random_shuffle(xs) // the same members, in a seeded order
1073-```
1074-
1075-`random_seed` makes the draws after it reproducible: the same seed gives the
1076-same sequence of draws, in one process or in two. A program that never seeds
1077-draws from a fixed default seed rather than from the clock, so Redblue's output
1078-is reproducible by default and a program has to *ask* for variation it cannot
1079-reproduce. A seed outside the range of a 64-bit machine integer has no bits left
1080-to give and takes the nearest bound, so `random_seed(1e300)` is the seed
1081-`random_seed(9223372036854775807)` names; the two ends are different seeds from
1082-each other. `random` answers a whole number and `random_number` a fraction; both
1083-refuse a range whose low end is above its high end, because such a range has no
1084-member. A range may be written with two arguments, with one — which is the high
1085-end, with zero as the low end, so `random(6)` is a die numbered zero through six
1086-and `random_number(10)` is `[0, 10)` — or with none, which is `[0, 100]` for
1087-`random` and `[0, 1)` for `random_number`. `random` answers one of the whole
1088-numbers in its range, so a fractional bound narrows the range rather than making
1089-the draw a fraction: `random(0, 100.5)` answers `0` to `100`, and a range holding
1090-no whole number at all, such as `random(0.2, 0.8)`, is refused. `random_choice`
1091-refuses an empty list, since it has no member to choose.
1092-
1093-### files
1094-
1095-```redblue
1096-set content to files.read("data.rb")
1097-might fail files.write("output.rb", content)
1098-might fail files.append("log.rb", "new line")
1099-if files.exists("config.rb")
1100-    // ...
1101-end
1102-```
1103-
1104-### list
1105-
1106-```redblue
1107-set doubled to list.map([1, 2, 3], to (x) give back x * 2)
1108-```
1109-
1110-`list.filter` and `list.reduce` are registered but not implemented. `list.map`
1111-is the only higher-order builtin, and it works in all three spellings:
1112-`map(xs, f)`, `list.map(xs, f)` and `xs.map(f)`.
1113-
1114-### network
1115-
1116-```redblue
1117-set response to network.get("https://api.example.com")
1118-set response to network.post("https://api.example.com", data)
1119-```
1120-
1121-Both calls block until the response arrives, so there is nothing to write in
1122-front of them. `wait` is an ordinary identifier, and the `wait`-prefixed spelling
1123-this section used to carry did not parse. See "Asynchronous Programming" above.
1124-
1125-### json and csv
1126-
1127-```redblue
1128-set obj to json.parse('{"name": "Alice"}')
1129-set text to json.stringify(obj)
1130-set rows to csv.parse("name,age\nAlice,30")
1131-```
1132-
1133-There is no `formats` module. JSON and CSV are their own modules, spelled
1134-`json.*` and `csv.*`.
1135-
1136-### bytes
1137-
1138-```redblue
1139-set data to bytes.from_text("Hi")        // [72, 105] — a byte list
1140-set back to bytes.text(data)             // "Hi"
1141-bytes.write("out.bin", data)             // write a byte list to a file
1142-```
1143-
1144-A byte is the whole number 0 to 255. `files.write` writes the UTF-8 encoding of
1145-a text, so `bytes` is how a program writes a byte it cannot spell — which a
1146-compiler writing a bytecode file cannot avoid. A value that is not a byte, or a
1147-byte list that is not valid UTF-8, is a refusal naming the byte rather than a
1148-wrapped value or a replacement character three files later.
1149-
1150-### sys
1151-
1152-```redblue
1153-for each argument in sys.argv()
1154-    say argument
1155-end
1156-```
1157-
1158-`sys.argv()` is the list of arguments after the program path, and it is empty
1159-for a program run from the REPL or from a test, because only `rb run` collects
1160-them.
1161-
1162-### time
1163-
1164-```redblue
1165-set stamp to time.now()                  // a record: seconds, nanoseconds
1166-time.sleep(0.5)                          // pause for half a second
1167-say time.format(1704067200, "%Y-%m-%d")  // "2024-01-01"
1168-say time.unix("2024-01-01 00:00:00")     // 1704067200
1169-```
1170-
1171-`time.now()` is a record of `seconds` and `nanoseconds` since
1172-1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and `time.sleep` answers `nothing` once the pause is over.
1173-`time.format` takes a number of seconds since the same instant and a format
1174-string; with no format string it writes `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`. `time.unix` is the
1175-other direction: it takes `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` and answers the seconds since
1176-the same instant, read as UTC whichever time zone the machine is in, so the
1177-answer is the same on the machine that wrote the text and on one that reads it.
1178-
1179-`time.sleep` is the one call in this section that blocks — the other three are
1180-computed and returned immediately, so `time.format` and `time.unix` cost no wall
1181-time whatever the clock says. A program that wants a timer rather than a pause
1182-has to read the clock itself — `time.now()` twice and `time.sleep` between them.
1183-The calls that block besides `time.sleep` are `network.get` and `network.post`,
1184-which wait for a response.
1185-
