1188-## Grammar
1189-
1190-See [GRAMMAR.md](GRAMMAR.md) for the complete EBNF grammar specification.
1191-
1192----
1193-
1194-## Appendix: Keywords Reference
1195-
1196-| Keyword | Description |
1197-|---------|-------------|
1198-| set | Declare/assign variable |
1199-| to | Assignment or function declaration |
1200-| is | Comparison |
1201-| nothing | Null value |
1202-| yes/no | Boolean literals |
1203-| if/then/else/end | Conditional statements |
1204-| unless | Negative condition |
1205-| for/each/in/from/times | Loop constructs |
1206-| while/repeat/until | Loop constructs |
1207-| break/skip | Loop control |
1208-| return/give back | Function return |
1209-| might fail | Error-prone operation |
1210-| constant | Declare a name that may not be rebound |
1211-| module/import/export/as | Module system |
1212-| and/or/not | Logical operators |
1213-| mod | Remainder |
1214-| try/catch/finally | Error handling |
1215-| object/has/can | Object declarations, properties, methods |
1216-| this/extends | Reference to the receiver; inheritance |
1217-| say/print | Output |
1218-| test/expect | Test blocks |
1219-
1220-A word that is not on this list is an ordinary identifier, including `are`,
1221-`ask`, `async`, `done`, `new`, `parallel`, `that`, `wait` and `when`. They were
1222-reserved by the lexer with no rule anywhere in the parser to read them, which
1223-made them unusable as names as well as unimplemented; they are identifiers again
1224-and the four forms that used to be promised for them are not part of the
1225-language. `until` is reserved and belongs to the `while`/`repeat` forms above.
1226-`takes`, `needs`, `called`, `either`, `neither`, `every`, `error`, `number`,
1227-`text`, `list` and `record` have never been reserved and are ordinary
1228-identifiers too.
1229-
1230----
1231-
1232-*This specification is a draft. Implementation details may change before v1.0.*
